### 3. FEBio Simulation

The irregular shape of the ultrasound probe produces uneven deformation across the cervix. The resulting changes in tissue shear-wave speed, which depend on probe shape and compression magnitude, can be simulated and matched to the elastogram to determine tissue properties. In this study, FEBio [24] was used to simulate compression. A 3D-scanned CAD model of the ultrasound probe indented a block of nearly incompressible tissue (52 × 40 × 35 mm) under static structural mechanics. Figure 1 shows the simulation, and Table 2 describes its elements. The applied indentation ranged from 0 to 20 mm.

The shear-wave speed in undeformed tissue was defined as the base tissue speed, \(c_{s0}\). At each indentation step, the principal stretches of every tissue element within the ultrasound frame of view were recorded. These stretches produced a proportional increase in the base speed. The proportional increase, \(P\), from the base speed, \(c_{s0}\), was calculated for stiffening parameters in the ranges \(0.01 < \alpha < 100\) and \(0.01 < C_2 < 100\), as shown in Equations (7)–(9). The proportional-increase field represents the increase from the base-speed value within the area of interest and does not depend on the base parameter.

\[
P=\frac{c_s}{c_{s0}}
\tag{7}
\]

\[
P_{\mathrm{DF}}=\lambda_2^2 e^{\alpha(I_1-3)}
\tag{8}
\]

\[
P_{\mathrm{VW}}=\lambda_2^2 e^{C_2(I_1-3)}
-\lambda_2^{-2\lambda_1^{-2}}
-\lambda_2^{-2\lambda_1^{-2}}
\tag{9}
\]

Here, \(\lambda_1\) denotes the principal stretch in the direction of probe indentation. The simulation showed that \(\lambda_1\) decreased, whereas \(\lambda_{2,3}\) increased, as indentation increased. Figure 2 shows the deformation pattern caused by the probe in polar coordinates.

The maximum change in \(\lambda\) occurred at the point of contact between the probe and the tissue. The effect spread outward from this point and decreased in magnitude with distance from the probe. At each threshold intensity, the area of effect, \(AOE_{\mathrm{thres}}\), was defined as the number of pixels for which the proportional increase from the base speed exceeded the threshold.

The magnitude of the change increased with indentation. Figure 3 shows the areas at different thresholds for increasing indentation levels. The thresholds ranged from 1.10 to 3.00 proportional increase from the base speed.

For efficient translation between clinical and simulated elastograms, the simulated tissue was converted from Cartesian to polar coordinates. Each pixel measured \(0.023^\circ\) and \(0.008\) mm. Figure 4 shows examples of the simulated proportional-increase field with traced \(AOE_{\mathrm{thres}}\) outlines.

Each proportional-increase field was unique to the material model, indentation, and stiffening parameter. The area of effect, \(AOE_{\mathrm{thres}}\), and the peak proportional increase, \(P_{\max}\), were used to define each field. \(P_{\max}\) was defined as the average proportional increase from the base speed along the central axis extending from the probe, where the magnitude of the principal-stretch change was greatest. Simulation-based fitting was used to determine the parameters of each elastogram. The measured \(AOE_{\mathrm{thres}}\) and \(P_{\max}\) values were used to calculate the indentation and stiffening parameters for each material model, as shown in Figures 5 and 6.

The simulation used two assumptions. First, cervical tissue was nearly incompressible. Second, the tissue parameters were constant within the measured area. Section 6 discusses the problems and limitations of these assumptions.
